{
  "evidence": "- TEXT_F: Recruit 20,000 nurses, 8,000 GPs, 3,000 midwives funded by a mansion tax/tobacco levy; mental-health parity and faster talking-therapy access; abolish the Bedroom Tax; reform the Work Capability Assessment; higher contributory JSA; raise the National Minimum Wage to >£8 and promote the Living Wage; ban exploitative zero‑hours contracts and give a right to a regular contract; cut tuition fees to £6,000; expand free childcare and double paternity leave; freeze energy bills and strengthen the regulator; build at least 200,000 homes with renter protections (three‑year tenancies, rent-rise ceilings, ban letting fees); restore the 50p top rate, abolish non‑dom status, close loopholes; gender pay-gap reporting, race‑equality strategy, advance LGBT rights; protect the Human Rights Act and ECHR; maintain 0.7% aid.\n- TEXT_E: Eliminate the deficit and run surpluses; two‑year freeze of most working‑age benefits and lower the household benefit cap; roll out Universal Credit and tighten conditionality (e.g., Youth Allowance with stricter work/training rules, restrict Housing Benefit for 18–21s); raise the higher‑rate tax threshold and increase the Inheritance Tax threshold on the family home to £1m; replace the Human Rights Act with a British Bill of Rights; target net migration in the tens of thousands with a 4‑year contribution rule for in‑work benefits; NHS spending up by at least £8bn and seven‑day services.",
  "reasoning": "Because the electorate’s top priorities are Care and Equality, the strong redistributive, welfare‑protective, workers’ rights, housing, healthcare, and rights‑preserving measures in TEXT_F align more closely with those foundations, while TEXT_E emphasizes proportionality/authority/loyalty via austerity, welfare caps, migration restrictions, and replacing the HRA. Although TEXT_E contains some care‑oriented NHS and childcare commitments, TEXT_F more consistently centers harm‑reduction and equalizing policies across domains.",
  "confidence": "high",
  "winner": "TEXT_F"
}
